there's been a lot of robots in movies  and our challenge was to do something  that was it was sort of fresh and unique  and in the comic books he took a couple  different forms he was at one point sort  of this bio mech cynthy dragon thing  that could turn also into a human being  with a bowler hat and but then he also  had a different kind of mech form and  while these were really really cool and  inventive I was concerned about how it  would fit in the world that we were  creating and so I the one of the first  research trip I did was a robotics  research trip and it was this it was the  week that Winnie the Pooh was released  in July of 2011 I was at MIT and Harvard  and Carnegie Mellon again just looking  for inspiration and one of our biz dev  artists coined the phrase huggable robot  she's like this robots gotta be huggable  sounds like alright she's gotta be  unique and huggable and he's got to be  everything so I you know I went on the  road and and did my research just  looking for inspiration in a huggable  robot and it was at Carnegie Mellon  University I met this guy by the name of  Chris Atkinson who is a researcher doing  research into soft robotics and I had  never heard of soft robotics before but  it blew me away and essentially what it  is is it's soft robots made of a  material like vinyl or something you  know pliable and filled with air and the  practical application for this  technology will be in the healthcare  industry in the future there'll be you  know robotic nurses and nurses aides and  that type of thing  and I had never seen anything like it  and Chris himself is actually a very  passionate roboticist much like we're  passionate about animation he's very  passionate about robotics and he started  going off on a bit of a rant and about  movies and how robots are portrayed in  movies and it's very frustrating to him  that robots are always the bad guy  it's always sort of you know technology  run amuck and and when no when is  somebody gonna put a robot on screen  that's the good guy that's the hero of  the story and it's like I finally  stopped and was like dude you had me at  vinyl trust me we won't let you down so  I was so taken by this technology that I  brought it back to the studio and said  art this is gonna be our robot and  and then it became about sort of  designing baymax and one of the other  things I learned on this trip was this  idea of the uncanny valley and it's  essentially like when machines or robots  specifically begin to get to human  looking they gets super creepy and we  didn't want baymax to be creepy we  wanted to be you know cool and nice and  gentle and compassionate so I had a  strong desire to keep him very simple in  design and also I felt like we have you  know some of the best animators on the  planet so we could give him you know a  paper bag and they'd be able to act with  it so and so I knew that they would be  up for the challenge so he never was  intended to have a face one of the  things we did think about is like did we  want him to have some sort of you know  screen where he might have a mouth that  would express or anything like that and  John wasn't too keen on the idea he just  thought it you know simpler you keep it  the better and so when I was in Tokyo I  was at a temple and I was looking up and  I saw these bells looking down at me and  I was awash with this feeling of  peacefulness and calm and that's kind of  what we wanted baymax to be to have this  effect on people and so snapped a couple  photos I just loved that idea of kind of  a circle with a line through it and I  brought it back to the studio and gave  it to our character designer she and Kim  and said there's his face he's like okay  Circle Circle Line done
 

